\begin{abstract}
We introduce the \emph{Polychromatic Traveling Salesman Problem} (PCTSP), where the input is an edge weighted graph whose vertices are partitioned into $k$ equal-sized color classes, and the goal is to find a minimum-length Hamiltonian cycle that visits the classes in a fixed cyclic order.  
This generalizes the Bipartite TSP (when $k = 2$) and the classical TSP (when $k = n$).  
We give a polynomial-time $(3 - 2\cdot10^{-36})$-approximation algorithm for metric PCTSP.  
Complementing this, we show that Euclidean PCTSP is APX-hard even in $\mathbb{R}^2$, ruling out the existence of a PTAS unless P = NP.
\end{abstract}

