\paragraph{Real Humans}
Arguably, the most concerning type of image recall are those which potentially contain a real human model. Several of the duplicates we identified indeed contain real humans that we could find their source, see \cref{tab:reconstruction-examples}.

As discussed, previous works also managed to extract real-humans \cite{carlini2023}. However this was generally done by prompts that actively request that person's image, and the focus was on copyright and verbatim copy.
%
Here, the person is extracted by a prompt that, seemingly, does not contain their name, but instead the prompt contains a collocation associated with a product that the model had presented in the image. For example, mundane items such as "T-Shirt".
Even in cases where the face was deformed (as often happens in SD) or the picture is only of the torso, it might still contain identifying markers such as tattoos as well as other specific visual attributes such as haircut and pose. This is exemplified in \cref{tab:interpolation} (left columns).

