\subsection{Print on Demand Review}\label{ssec:PoD}
Print-on-Demand (PoD) is a business model that allows sellers to create and sell custom-designed products—such as T-shirts, posters, mugs, and phone cases—without maintaining inventory. When a customer places an order, the item is printed and shipped by a third-party service, eliminating the need for bulk production. This model has become increasingly popular due to low upfront costs, scalability, and integration with e-commerce platforms like Etsy, Shopify, and Amazon.

The PoD market has grown rapidly, with a global valuation of \$6.18 billion in 2022. Leading PoD platforms such as Redbubble, Teespring, Society6, Printful, and Zazzle allow independent sellers to upload and showcase designs, often generating multiple product mockups from a single artwork. 

To streamline the selling process, PoD platforms use automated design placement tools that overlay artwork onto pre-existing product images using smart masks and blending techniques. One such noticeable platform is Printful, whose output images are integrated into many other websites. This eliminates the need for sellers to take real promotional photos, as the system can instantly generate realistic previews showing the design on different product types, angles, and lighting conditions. As a result, such websites display many images that are identical up to a fixed region where the design is places. 

Due to their popularity, PoD platforms host millions of publicly viewable product images. These images frequently appear in search engine results, social media promotions, and web crawlers, increasing their visibility across the internet. The automated nature of mockup generation further amplifies the number of PoD-related images online, as a single mockup may appear along with many design variations.

The LAION-5B dataset \cite{schuhmann2022laion5b}, a large-scale, web-scraped image-text dataset, likely contains a significant number of PoD-generated images due to their widespread presence on the internet. It's difficult to determine the exact predominance of images coming from PoD mockups on LAION-5B, but it is clearly significant. Further analysis of dataset composition could help assess the extent of PoD imagery and its impact on generative AI and computer vision applications.

\subsection{PoD contribution to Template Memorization} \label{ssec:PoD contribution}
Webster \cite{webster2023} wrote that "Template verbatims are more difficult to label as ground truth, not only because they require retrieval and masking, but also because they’re not highly duplicated.". We believe that the training images that lead to template memorization are highly duplicated in a template, rather than exact way, meaning that the images are duplicated in the fixed region, paired with captions containing their associated collocations. Methods searching for exact duplicates are indifferent to this sense of duplication, making them robust to de-duplication effort made on exact duplicated.

We note that due to posting in different platforms detailed in \ref{ssec:PoD}, some of the image-text pairs are duplicated in the exact sense in addition to template duplication. Those samples could be removed from the dataset by exact de-duplication methods.

Testing this hypothesis on LAION requires immense computational resources which we did not hold, for searching LAION along with image masking and regex, making it impossible to use the embedding space directly such as done with computationally and memory efficient methods such as FAISS.

In order to support our claim, without accessing LAION we create intentional template memorization by creating a dataset of images with design placement as done in PoD websites and matching captions containing the same collocations with different descriptors matching the placed design, and than fine-tune Stable Diffusion on this datasets to show how the generated images behave as TMIs. \ref{ssec:lora}

Another observation that we make is about the contribution of the data from such e-commerce websites to the constructions of the template groups associating equivalent collocations with multiple image templates. Typically in these websites multiple images of the same product appear together under the product description that would give the caption in the dataset, such images describe the same product and print on 3-5 mockups depicting different background and angles, or even slight variations of the same product such as a phone case fitting different phone models.

