\section{Observed Phenomena in Template-Memorized Images} \label{sec:phenomena}

By reconstructing many template-memorized images, we were able to observe the following phenomena, which, to the best of our knowledge, were not previously discussed in literature. 

\subsection{Interpolation} \label{ssec:interpolation}
An important type of image recreation we observed involves interpolation of several real-world images. Several images that were generated by our process were not necessarily verbatim copy of an existing image, nor did they involve naive copying of the background or foreground. Instead, we could find that certain elements were copied from different sources in a single image. This phenomena is demonstrated in \cref{tab:interpolation}. Such type of copying can be challenging to detect. For example, one of the images that can be seen in \cref{tab:interpolation} copies a tattoo that appeared in a real-world image, but not the whole image. That means that the copied image and the copying image might not be close at all at any metric, and yet it is clear that certain elements in the picture were blatantly copied. This also raises important questions about the level of granularity at which copying should be defined and evaluated. We speculate that some generated images may be interpolated from numerous source images, making them difficult, or even infeasible, to trace with current methods and resources.

