\subsection{Perturbations}\label{ssec:perturbations}
We’ve noticed clusters of images that were nearly identical in the template sense but with one or more objects perturbed between semantically similar objects in a fixed location such as lamps and chairs. This is exemplified in \cref{fig:perturbations}. Such perturbations preserve a high CLIP similarity (outside the editable region) between the images. Perturbations somewhat affect the pixel-wise $\ell_2$ similarity since they change the pixel values, but still maintain a higher-than-random similarity. 
%
While difficult to pinpoint the source of this phenomena, it stands in concurrence with the expected consequences of minor perturbations in the noise latent space, as demonstrated in \cite{kerastutorial}.
It might also stem from the one-step denoising behavior described by \cite{webster2023}, as a clear layout of the image after the first step, leaves room only for minor changes in the next steps.

