Figure~\ref{fig:metrics} presents the joint distribution of key performance metrics such as throughput, PDR, jitter, and end-to-end delay over the full range of fractal dimensions. Networks with \( D \geq 5 \) consistently occupy regions associated with superior performance, forming compact clusters characterized by high throughput, high PDR, and low latency and jitter. In contrast, lower-dimensional networks (\( D < 5 \)) appear more scattered and exhibit substantially degraded performance. In particular, the network at \( D = 1 \) exhibits poor performance. Both throughput and PDR exhibit notable declines, while delay and jitter increase, showing poor communication. This reflects the fragmented topology (Figure~\ref{fig:topology}) characterized by poor linking and disjoint clusters.

