\subsubsection*{Broader Impact Statement}

Our work makes several contributions: i) we propose a theoretical analysis using a square-root parameterization for the Gaussian covariance, which we expect can encourage further research into establishing convergence guarantees for natural-gradient variational inference, particularly for cases involving concave log-likelihoods. ii) Our experimental results reveal that natural gradient methods outperform algorithms relying on Euclidean or Wasserstein geometries, showcasing their practical advantages in terms of convergence speed and effectiveness, which could impact the development of more robust inference algorithms in probabilistic modeling.


