There are a few IWAs that have a noticeably large gap between the fraction of positive feedback when they are a user goal vs.\ an AI action. Interestingly, the two largest are \emph{Provide support or encouragement to others} and \emph{Advise others on products or services}. When the AI tries to directly provide support or advice, people are less satisfied than when it helps them provide support or advice to others. The GWA-level analysis (\Cref{fig:thumb-rate-top-bottom-gwas}) also shows that activities involving doing things for others (coaching, providing advice, and interpreting things) stand out with high shares of positive feedback, all with even higher satisfaction when the AI helps the user do them than when it tries to do them itself.   

To supplement thumbs feedback, we also look at which work activities have the highest and lowest completion rates, as described in \Cref{sec:completion}. Relative to the thumbs data, this has the disadvantage of not reflecting the user's opinion, but the advantage of avoiding selection in which users give feedback (which is why we use completion in our AI applicability score). We find that there is a strong correlation between the positive feedback fraction for an IWA and its completion rate (weighted $r=0.83$ for user goal IWAs and $r=0.76$ for AI action IWAs, filtering out IWAs below activity share 0.05\%; see \Cref{fig:thumbs-vs-completion}). Moreover, we find very high consistency between IWA completion rates measured in \unif and \thumbs (weighted $r > 0.9$, see \Cref{fig:completion-uniform-vs-thumbs}). At the conversation level in \thumbs, the correlation between whether a conversation received a thumbs up and whether it was classified as completing the user's task is $r = 0.28$, indicating they are related but that the relationship is noisier before aggregating to IWAs. \Cref{fig:completion-rate-top-bottom-iwas} shows the top and bottom IWAs by completion rate, which mostly shows similar patterns as the top and bottom IWAs by thumbs feedback, with the addition of advice and explanation IWAs having high completion rates. 


\paragraph{Scope of impact.}
In addition to success within a conversation, another crucial aspect of work impact is the extent to which the AI capability demonstrated in the conversation translates to the work represented by an IWA. As described in \Cref{sec:task-weights}, we use our measure of impact scope to identify which IWAs are most deeply affected by demonstrated AI capabilities. \Cref{fig:scope-top-bottom-iwas} shows the IWAs with highest and lowest impact scope; as with satisfaction and completion, the most deeply impacted IWAs include gathering information and writing, as well as providing information, advising, and explaining on the AI side. Low impact scope IWAs again include data analysis and visual design, but also others about interacting with external people (e.g., \iwa{Confer with clients}, \iwa{Coordinate with others}, \iwa{Investigate individuals}, \iwa{Verify personal information}). Notably, we find consistently lower impact scope on the AI action side than the user goal side: our data indicates that AI can help users with a broader fraction of their work than it can perform directly.  Supporting the notion that scope measures something different from completion, we find that scope is much less correlated with completion than satisfaction is (weighted $r = 0.45$ and $r = 0.22$; see \Cref{fig:scope-vs-completion}). On the other hand, of these three measures, IWA scope is the best predictor of which activities people seek AI assistance with most often ($r = 0.64$ with log user goal activity share; see \Cref{fig:scope-vs-activity-share}). That is, people are using LLMs for the tasks for which the LLM can have broadest impact (but not necessarily the ones the LLM completes most successfully). 


\subsection{Occupations}

\Cref{tab:top-occupations} shows the 40 occupations with the highest AI applicability score as defined by \Cref{eq:ai_score}. Recall that our AI applicability score combines, for each occupation, whether Copilot users are performing its associated work activities (frequency $>.05\%$) successfully (completion rate) and covering a broad share of the work activity (scope $\geq$ moderate). (See \Cref{sec:task-weights} and \Cref{eq:ai_score} for more details.) 
Interpreters and Translators are at the top of the list, with 98\% of their work activities overlapping with frequent Copilot tasks with fairly high completion rates and scope scores. Other occupations with high applicability scores include those related to writing/editing, sales, customer service, programming, and clerking.   Along with Interpreters and Translators, there are myriad other knowledge work occupations such as Historians, Writers and Authors, CNC Tool Programmers, Brokerage Clerks, Political Scientists, Reporters and Journalists, Mathematicians, Proofreaders, Editors, PR Specialists, etc. 
By contrast, \Cref{tab:bottom-occupations} shows the 40 occupations with the lowest AI applicability scores. 
The least-impacted occupations include occupations that require physically working with people (e.g., Nursing Assistants, Massage Therapists), operating or monitoring machinery (e.g., Water Treatment Plant and Systems Operators, Pile Driver Operators, Truck and Tractor Operators), and other manual labor  (e.g., Dishwashers, Roofers, Maids and Housekeeping Cleaners). Note that our measurement is purely about LLMs: other applications of AI could certainly affect occupations involving operating and monitoring machinery, such as truck driving. 


