In our CSR based design, we define hashmaps: one where the key is the label and the value is a Node, and another where the key is an edge label and the value is a linked list of edges that contain this label.
It is worth mentioning that, so far, we have shown how the graph is stored, but not how to work with paths. Since the logical and physical operators in PathDB handle sets of paths, we need to transform the nodes and edges of a graph $G$ into sets of paths. To achieve this, two main functions are used: $\paths_0(G)$, which retrieves the paths of length 0 of a graph $G$, and $\paths_1(G,l)$, which retrieve the paths of length 1 with a label $l$ from $G$.



\paragraph{Path Algebra}
\label{palg}
Our path algebra~\cite{EDBT.2025.63} defines five core operators that operate over sets of paths. The \textbf{selection} operator ($\sigma$) filters paths by applying conditions over nodes or edges. The \textbf{union} operator ($\cup$) merges two sets of paths, $a$ and $b$, into a single set. The  \textbf{join} operator ($\bowtie^{\tau}$) concatenates paths from set $a$ with compatible paths from set $b$; the join may optionally be constrained by a \textit{path semantic} $\tau$, which can be one of \code{isTrail} for no repeated edges, \code{isAcyclic} for no repeated nodes, or \code{isSimple} for no repeated nodes, except the first and last node. Finally, the \textbf{recursive} operator ($\phi^{\tau}$) applies repeated join operations to compute transitive expansions of a path set, either until a fix point is reached or until specific criteria are satisfied. Like the join, the recursive operator can also be constrained by a \textit{path semantic} $\tau$, allowing it to generate only trails, simple, or acyclic paths when required. Finally, the \textbf{projection} operator allows us to extract specific components from the resulting paths and optionally limit the number of results returned.
These operators form the foundation for expressing rich navigational queries. An example that demonstrates the combined application of selection, union, join,  recursion, and projection is presented in Figure~\ref{fig:basicgraph},  shaped as an execution tree.

\paragraph{Path Query Pattern}
\label{pqp}
A path query pattern is an expression inspired by the GQL standard~\cite{2024iso}, and consists of three main components: the \code{MATCH} clause, that indicate the path pattern, the \code{WHERE} clause, that indicate the selection conditions, and the \code{RETURN} clause, that indicates the output terms.

{
\begin{verbatim}
        MATCH <restrictor>? <var> = <pathPattern> 
        (WHERE <conditions>)? 
        RETURN <term>+ <limit>?
\end{verbatim}
}

The \code{MATCH} clause consists of three components: a path \code{restrictor} (\code{WALK}, \code{TRAIL}, \code{ACYCLIC}, or \code{SIMPLE}), a path variable \code{var} (a symbolic identifier formed by the concatenation of characters), and a path pattern \code{pathPattern}. The path pattern has the form \code{(x)-[r]->(y)}, where \code{x} and \code{y} represent the source and target variables (these can be explicitly named and later referenced in subsequent clauses), and \code{r} is a regular expression over the edge labels of the graph. Let $r_1$ and $r_2$ be regular expressions. Valid constructions for \code{r} include: a label $r_1$; a negated label $!r_1$; concatenation $r_1 r_2$; alternation $r_1 \mid r_2$; Kleene star $r_1^*$; positive closure $r_1^+$; and the optional expression $r_1?$.

The \code{WHERE} clause contains a selection condition  used to filter the paths retrieved in the \code{MATCH} clause. Let $i \geq 1$ be a natural number, \code{pr} a property label, $v$ a value, $\oplus$ a comparison operator ($=$, $\neq$, $<$, $>$, $\leq$, $\geq$), and \code{sVar} and \code{tVar} symbolic variables representing the source and target nodes of a path pattern. A \emph{selection condition} $c$ is defined recursively. A  selection condition in PathDB can be any expression such as:

\begin{multicols}{3}
    \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
      \item \texttt{sVar.pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{tVar.pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{FIRST().pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LAST().pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{NODE(i).pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{EDGE(i).pr} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(sVar)} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(tVar)} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(FIRST())} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(LAST())} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(NODE(i))} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LABEL(EDGE(i))} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
      \item \texttt{LENGTH()} $\oplus$ \texttt{v}
    \end{itemize}
\end{multicols}

Additionally boolean predicates such as \code{ISTRAIL()}, \code{ISSIMPLE()}, and \code{ISACYCLIC()} can be applied to filter path results. If $c_1$ and $c_2$ are selection conditions, then $(c_1 \code{ AND } c_2)$ and $(c_1 \code{ OR } c_2)$ are complex selection conditions.

Finally, the \code{RETURN} clause is used to specify the elements of the path to be projected in the result. Projection expressions may reference any variable defined in the \code{MATCH} clause, including path and node variables. Let $i \geq 1$ be a natural number, \code{pr} a property label, \code{Var} a path pattern variable, and \code{sVar} and \code{tVar} the symbolic identifiers of the source and target nodes of that path. A PathDB projection term can refer to node or edge attributes using expressions such as \code{sVar.pr}, \code{tVar.pr}, \code{NODE(i).pr}, \code{EDGE(i).pr}, \code{FIRST().pr}, and \code{LAST().pr}. It can also include functions that extract structural or semantic features of the path, such as \code{LABEL()}, \code{LENGTH()}, \code{ISTRAIL()}, \code{ISSIMPLE()}, and \code{ISACYCLIC()}. An optional \code{LIMIT} clause may be appended to restrict the number of returned results.


\paragraph{System Design}

The PathDB design is structured around three primary components, reflecting its query evaluation workflow, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:queryflow}: the parser, the logical planner, and the physical planner.

First, the  parser is responsible for validation, extraction, and transformation of a path query pattern into an abstract syntax tree (AST). This process is carried out using ANTLR4\footnote{ANTLR (ANother Tool for Language Recognition), https://www.antlr.org} , a tool that, given a specific grammar (in this case, the grammar of a path pattern query for PathDB), converts the query into an AST. This AST has three main components: the source node, the regular expression, and the target node.

Second, the AST, specifically the regular expression, serves as the input for the logical planner component. The AST is then transformed into a logical plan tree. Essentially, we convert the path query pattern into multiple algebraic operators. It is worth noting that our logical plan tree is evaluated in a bottom-up manner, from the leaves to the root.

For example, to retrieve paths that start at a with a property  $name=Moe$ and traverse one or more repetitions of either a \code{Knows} or \code{Likes} edge followed by a \code{HasCreator} edge, a user can formulate the query: 

\code{MATCH TRAIL p = (x)-[(Knows|Likes.HasCreator)+]->(y) WHERE x.name = "Moe" RETURN y.name LIMIT 10}.

The above query is based on the graph of Figure \ref{fig:basicgraph}, the corresponding logical plan is presented in Figure \ref{fig:subfigA}. This logical plan specifies the operators required to evaluate the query: it first retrieves all edges from the graph $G$ labeled with \code{Knows}, \code{Likes} and \code{HasCreator}, and then transforms these edges into a set of paths. 
If we evaluate the expression from the left-hand side of the union, a recursive operator is applied over the set of paths labeled with \code{Likes}. On the right-hand side, a recursive operator is applied over the \code{Join} of two path sets: one labeled with \code{Likes} and the other with \code{HasCreator}. It is important to note that the \textit{path semantic} \code{isTrail} is enforced on the resulting paths—both for the recursion and for the join—ensuring that no edge is repeated during evaluation.

Above the union operator, the logical plan applies a selection that retains only paths whose source node has the property \texttt{"Name = Moe"}. Finally, a projection operator extracts the names associated with the last node of each remaining path, limiting the output to the top 10 results.


As can be observed, since we are working with a tree structure where each operation processes a set of paths, various optimizations can be applied during execution, which could improve execution times. 
%These optimizations are discussed further later on.

Finally, the physical plan implements the necessary algorithms to execute the operations specified by the logical plan, retrieving the corresponding results from the graph. To achieve this, each logical operator in the logical plan is translated into a corresponding physical operator algorithm. In this case, the physical plan operates on the graph stored in memory, processing each result on demand.

The design of PathDB allows for the isolation of these three components, thereby enabling optimizations or modifications to be made to each one without causing significant issues for the rest of the operator. This isolation, for instance, allows for optimizations in the logical plan, changes to the physical plan, such as moving from memory storage to disk storage or any other system, like a database, among other possible modifications.
