\subsection{LLM-Based Methods}
We evaluate tutor move and student outcome prediction using two large language model (LLM)-based methods.
% LLMs offer a powerful framework for analyzing and generating naturalistic dialogue, making them well-suited for studying instructional strategies within tutor-student interactions. In both methodologies, the input is structured as a sequential dialogue with special tokens marking the beginning and end of each interaction.
First, we fine-tune the \textbf{Llama} 3.2 3B model using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) \cite{saari2018lora}, where we train the model to predict the labels as text tokens following the input, using comma separation for multi-label turns.
Second, we use zero-shot prompting with \textbf{GPT-4o}, where we prompt the model to follow the annotation schema in each particular dataset to identify and predict tutor moves. We do not use GPT-4o for student outcome prediction because pre-trained LLMs do not show high alignment with student behavior \cite{liu2025llmsmakemistakeslike}. We show an example prompt in Figure \ref{fig:prompt-full}, and provide further implementation details in Appendix \ref{sec:appendix-experiments}. In order to determine how tutor move information impacts LLM predictions, for both methods, we experiment with including only the dialogue as input, as well as both the dialogue and previous turn move labels as input.
% \as{need more detail - we use next token prediction to predict moves and correctness, for multi-label moves comma separate labels} 
