% \textbf{Correlating Interestingness with Sophistication and Logical Depth.}  
% A high \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \) indicates that increasing the token count substantially improves reconstruction quality, suggesting that the tokenizer uncovers additional structure in the image. Conversely, a low \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \) implies that either the structure is trivially captured with few tokens or that the image lacks any meaningful structure. By normalizing with the best achievable reconstruction (\( \text{SSIM}_\text{high}^2 \)), the interestingness score \( \kappa \) becomes low for simple images (where \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \approx 0 \), \( \text{SSIM}_\text{high} \approx 1 \)) and becomes artificially high for noisy images (where \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \approx 0 \), but \( \text{SSIM}_\text{high} \ll 1 \)). In contrast, images with rich internal structure exhibit moderate \( \kappa \) values - mirroring the behavior of theoretically sophisticated objects in Algorithmic Information Theory, which lie in the intermediate Kolmogorov Complexity region. Thus, \textbf{\( \kappa \) serves as an approximate but practical proxy for sophistication.} If the loss in reconstruction quality is nominal with the change in tokens, the image can be represented with fewer tokens $\implies$ fewer self-attention operations $\implies$ smaller computational budget to decompress, and thus \textbf{alignment of $\kappa$ with logical depth.}

% \textbf{Correlating Interestingness with Sophistication and Logical Depth.}  
% A high \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \) indicates that increasing the token count substantially improves reconstruction quality, suggesting that the tokenizer is uncovering additional structure in the image. Conversely, a low \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \) implies that either the structure is trivially captured with few tokens or that the image lacks any meaningful structure. By normalizing with the best achievable reconstruction metric (\( \text{SSIM}_\text{high}^2 \)), the interestingness score \( \kappa \) becomes low for simple images (where \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \approx 0 \), \( \text{SSIM}_\text{high} \approx 1 \)) and becomes artificially high for noisy images (where \( \Delta_\text{SSIM} \approx 0 \), but \( \text{SSIM}_\text{high} \ll 1 \)).  This contrast with images having rich internal structure and hence exhibit moderate \( \kappa \) values—distinct from both extremes—\textit{correlating with the sophistication measure}. To make \( \kappa \) a more reliable, monotonically increasing estimate—like true sophistication—we adjust it using the statistical distribution (mean/variance) of observed \( \kappa \) values, multiplying the initial estimate by its statistical likelihood. This ensures that the statistically-aligned \( \kappa \) remains close to zero for both simple images (with low initial estimates) and highly noisy images (which lie in the long tail). All \( \kappa \) results hereafter are aligned using ImageNet-based statistics.  

% If the loss in reconstruction quality is nominal despite increasing the token count, the image can be represented with fewer tokens \( \Rightarrow \) fewer self-attention operations \( \Rightarrow \) lower computational cost for decompression—hence, an \textit{alignment of \( \kappa \) with the definition of logical depth}.

\vspace{-3mm}
\paragraph{Factors influencing learned estimates of minimum token count or Kolmogorov Complexity:}  
The proposed measure of image complexity depends on how well the tokenizer maps an image to its learned codebook space. Consequently, the estimates are influenced by whether the input is \textit{in-distribution (IID)} or \textit{out-of-distribution (OOD)}. For example, in Fig.~\ref{fig:kc_kappa_factors}, the dog and chessboard images illustrate this difference: despite the chessboard having a lower true KC, a tokenizer trained on ImageNet compresses the dog image more rigorously due to its higher similarity to the training distribution. Although this is an advantageous property of adaptive tokenizers—distinguishing IID from OOD data—the gap with true KC, in terms of assigning fewer tokens to simpler structures, can be reduced by training on larger datasets.

Another key factor is the amount of \textit{structure versus noise} in the image. Like theoretical KC, our learned complexity measure is sensitive to noise—Kolmogorov Complexity does not distinguish between a highly structured image and pure noise; both can exhibit high complexity. Although higher-order AIT concepts such as \textit{Sophistication} and \textit{Logical Depth} aim to make this distinction, a detailed exploration of them is beyond our current scope. A preliminary way to separate structure from noise using adaptive tokenizers could be to analyze how image reconstruction metrics (e.g., SSIM, $\ell_1$) change with the number of tokens. For example, while KARL assigns the maximum token count (256) to both a chessboard and a noise image, SSIM improves significantly with token count for the chessboard due to its simple, redundant, and identifiable patterns—whereas SSIM remains largely unchanged for pure noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:kc_kappa_factors}). Aligned with the theoretical concepts of sophistication and logistic depth, more interesting images are those that exhibit a mix of predictable and unpredictable structure, often resulting in intermediate values of $\Delta$ reconstruction with increasing tokens. For instance, images like dogs or Mandelbrot sets (Fig.~\ref{fig:kc_kappa_factors} rows 3,4) are more interesting to KARL than a simple chessboard or pure noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:kc_kappa_factors} rows 1,2). This suggests that the rate of improvement in reconstruction quality as the token budget increases can serve as a heuristic for identifying meaningful structure, further reinforcing the potential alignment between adaptive tokenizers and AIT. A deeper investigation into these higher-order AIT concepts represents a promising direction for future research.



