\begin{abstract}
    A family of sets satisfies the $(p,2)$-property if among any $p$ sets in the family, some two intersect. Two recent works used elaborate geometric techniques to show that any family of non-piercing regions in the plane that satisfies the $(p,2)$-property can be pierced by $O(p^9)$ points. In this note we show that even in a much more general setting, piercing by $O(p)$ points can be deduced from known results on hypergraphs with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph, which include intersection hypergraphs of non-piercing regions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A family $\F$ of simple Jordan regions in $\Re^2$ is called \emph{a pseudo-discs family} if any two boundaries of memebers of $\F$ intersect at most twice. A generalization of this notion was presented in \cite{Raman2020a}: A family of regions in $\Re^2$ is \emph{non-piercing} if for every $F,G \in \F$, $G \setminus F$ is connected.

A family $\F$ satisfies the $(p,q)$-property if among any $p$ elements of $\F$ some $q$ have non empty intersection. The celebrated $(p,q)$-theorem of Alon and Kleitman \cite{Alon1992} (see also \cite{Keller2018}) asserts that  for $p \geq q \geq d+1$ there exists $f=f(p,q,d)$ such that any family of compact convex sets in $\Re^d$ that satisfies the $(p,q)$-property, can be pierced by $f(p,q,d)$ points.
The $(p,q)$-theorem has a rich history of variations and generalizations (see, e.g., \cite{Alon2002, Alon1997,Barany2014}).
 For $q=2$ and $d \geq 2$, the general $(p,q)$-theorem does not hold. However, in several settings a $(p,2)$-theorem exists, e.g., for axis-parallel boxes in $\Re^d$ \cite{Corr15,Karolyi1991}, for translates \cite{Kim2006a} and homothets \cite{Dumitrescu2011} of a convex set in $\Re^d$, and more. 

Another interpretation of a $(p,2)$-theorem is bounding the transversal number of the family in terms of its packing number.
Due to this interpretation, a  $(p,2)$-theorem for any family of pseudo-discs follows from \cite[Theorem 4.6]{Chan2012} and independently from \cite{Pinchasi2015}. In both results, the size of the transversal is linear in $p$. 

Two recent papers \cite{Huang25,Pal25} proved $(p,2)$-theorems for non-piercing regions with transversal sizes polynomial in $p$ (the smaller size, obtained in \cite{Huang25}, is $O(p^9)$). In Observation \ref{obs:O(p)} below we show that these results, together with a recent work of Dalal et al.~\cite{Dalal24}, imply a $(p,2)$-theorem for non-piercing regions with a linear-sized transversal. 

More interestingly, we show that an almost linear $(p,2)$-theorem follows from a combination of several known results, even in the more general setting of hypergraphs with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph, defined as follows.

\begin{definition}
	The Delaunay graph of a hypergraph $H=(V,\E)$ is a graph $G$ on the vertex set $V$ whose edges are $\{e \in \E: |e|=2\}$.
\end{definition}

We say that a hypergraph $H=(V,\E)$ admits a hereditarily $c$-linear Delaunay graph for $c>0$, if for every induced subhypergraph $H{\mid}_{S}$ ($S \subset V$), the corresponding Delaunay graph $G_S$ satisfies $|E(G_S)| < c \cdot |S|$. If $c$ is constant, we simply say that the Delaunay graph is \emph{hereditarily linear}, and suppress the dependence on $c$.
The following definition will also be useful:

\begin{definition}
	Let $H=(V,\E)$ be a hypergraph. Two vertices $u,v \in V$ are \emph{friends} if there exists some $e \in \E$ such that $u,v \in e$.
\end{definition}

The following claim is an asymptotically optimal `fractional Helly theorem' for hypergraphs with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:1}
	Let $H=(V,\E)$ be a hypergraph, $|V|=n$, with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph, and let $0 < \alpha <1$. If $\alpha \binom{n}{2}$ of the pairs in $\binom{V}{2}$ are friends, then there exists $e \in \E$ such that $|e|>\beta n$, where $\beta = O(\alpha)$.
\end{theorem}

Given a set $P \subset \Re^d$ of points, and a family $\F$ of regions in $\Re^d$, the associated primal hypergraph is $H=(P,\E_{\F})$ where $\E_{\F}=\{ e_f=\{p \in P: p \in f\}\}_{f \in \F}$. The dual hypergraph of $\F$ is the hypergraph whose vertices are the elements of $\F$ and for every $x \in \Re^d$, $
e_x=\{B \in \F : x \in B\}$ is a hyperedge. The following $(p,2)$-theorem holds for hypergraphs with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:2}
	Let $\F$ be a family of $n$ regions in $\Re^d$ and let $H$ be the associated dual hypergraph. Assume that $H$ admits a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph. If $\F$ satisfies the $(p,2)$-property then $\F$ admits an $O(p \log p)$-sized transversal.
\end{theorem}

If this dual hypergraph admits a linear-sized epsilon-net (see definition in Section \ref{sec:main}), the assertion of Theorem \ref{thm:2} can be strengthened. In particular, the following holds.
\begin{corollary}\label{cor:3}($(p,2)$-theorem for non-piercing regions with a linear-sized transversal).
	Let $\F$ be a family of non-piercing regions that satisfies the $(p,2)$-property. Then $\F$ admits a transversal of size $O(p)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The proofs} \label{sec:main}
First, we observe that the existence of some $(p,2)$-theorem for families of non-piercing regions (as in \cite{Huang25,Pal25}), together with the following lemma of Dalal et al. \cite[based on \cite{Pinchasi2014}]{Dalal24}, implies a $(p,2)$-theorem with a linear-sized transversal in this setting.

\begin{lemma}\cite[Theorem 3]{Dalal24}\label{lem:157}
	Each family $\F$ of non-piercing regions contains some region $B \in \F$ such that the family $\{  A: A \in \F, A \cap B \neq \emptyset\}$ has the (157,2)-property.
\end{lemma}

\begin{observation}\label{obs:O(p)}
	Any family $\F$ of non-piercing regions with the $(p,2)$-property admits a transversal of size $O(p)$.
\end{observation}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Observation \ref{obs:O(p)}]
	By Lemma \ref{lem:157}, there exists $B \in \F$ such that the set $\F_1=\{ A: A \in \F , A \cap B \neq \emptyset\}$ satisfies the (157,2)-property. By \cite{Pal25} or \cite{Huang25}, $\F_1$ can be pierced by $C$ points, for some fixed constant $C$. Removing $\F_1$, the remaining family $\F \setminus \F_1$, satisfies the $(p-1,2)$-property. We continue in the same manner at most $p-1$ times, and obtain a transversal of $\F$ that contains at most $C(p-1)$ points. 
\end{proof}

A direct approach can be used to obtain a linear or almost linear $(p,2)$-theorem even in a more general setting. The direct approach goes through Theorem \ref{thm:1} which is a fractional Helly theorem for hypergraphs with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph. In order to prove Theorem \ref{thm:1}, we need the following lemma that follows from a result of Ackerman et al.~\cite[Lemma 22]{AKP21}:

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:friends}
	Let $H=(V,\E)$ be a hypergraph with hereditarily $c$-linear Delaunay graph. Let $k=\max_{e \in \E}|e|$ and let $X=\{  \{ p,q\} : p,q \in V \mbox{ are friends} \}$. Then $|X|=O(nk)$.
\end{lemma}

Lemma \ref{lem:friends} can be proved using a standard probabilistic argument. Form a random subset $V' \subset V$ by picking each vertex of $V$ independently with probability $x$. Let $G'$ be the corresponding Delaunay graph of $H{\mid}_{V'}$. By passing to the expection we obtain $(1 - x)^{k-2} x^2 |X| \leq \mathbb{E} ( | E(G')|) \leq c x n$, and for $x = \frac{1}{k-2}$ this yields $|X|=O(nk)$ as asserted.

\medskip \noindent Now we are ready to prove Theorem \ref{thm:1}. 
\begin{proof}[Proof of Throrem \ref{thm:1}]
	Let $k=\max_{e \in \E}|e|$. Let $X=\{ \{p,q\} : p,q \in V \mbox{ are friends}   \}$. Then by assumption, $|X| \geq \alpha \binom{n}{2}$ and by Lemma \ref{lem:friends}, $|X|=O(nk)$. It follows that $k=\Omega (\alpha n)$, and the assertion follows.
\end{proof}

Before proving the $(p,2)$-theorem (Theorem \ref{thm:2}), let us mention two ingredients from the Alon-Kleitman proof of the $(p,q)$-theorem \cite{Alon1992}. The first of them is a linear-programming duality lemma.

\medskip \noindent \textbf{LP duality lemma.} Let $0 < \alpha <1$ and let $\F$ be a family of sets such that for any multiset $\F'$ of elements of $\F$, there exists a point stabbing at least $\alpha |\F'|$ elements of $\F'$. Then there exists a finite multiset $Q$ such that each member of $\F$ contains at least $\alpha |Q|$ points of $Q$.  

The second ingredient we need from the Alon-Kleitman proof is the weak $\epsilon$-net theorem. In the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:2} below, we replace its use by a stronger theorem -- the $\epsilon$-net theorem.  

For $\epsilon>0$, an $\epsilon$-net of a hypergraph $H=(V,\E)$ is a set of vertices $S \subset V$ such that for any $e \in \E$ with $|e|>\epsilon|V|$, we have $S \cap e \neq \emptyset$.

\medskip \noindent \textbf{The $\epsilon$-net theorem (\cite{Haussler1987a},\cite{Komlos1992b}).} Let $\epsilon>0$. Any hypergraph $H=(V,\E)$ with VC-dimension $d$ admits an $\epsilon$-net of size at most $O \left(\frac{d}{\epsilon} \log \frac{1}{\epsilon} \right)$.

\medskip Like in the Alon-Kleitman proof, we deduce the $(p,2)$-theorem by combining the existence of a deep point that follows from Theorem \ref{thm:1} together with the LP-duality lemma and the $\epsilon$-net theorem.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:2}]
	Tur\'{a}n's theorem, together with the $(p,2)$-property of $\F$, imply that at least $\Omega(\frac{n^2}{p})$ pairs of sets in $\F$ intersect. Then by applying Theorem \ref{thm:1} to $H$, we get that there exists $x \in \Re^d$ that is contained in $\Omega(\frac{n}{p})$ sets of $\F$. Note that every multiset $\F'$ of elements of $\F$ satisfies the $(p,2)$-property as well, and hence the LP-duality lemma implies the existence of a finite multiset $Q$ of points in $\Re^d$, such that each member of $\F$ contains at least $\frac{|Q|}{p}$ points from $Q$.

	Note that $H$ has a bounded VC-dimension, being a hypergraph with a hereditarily linear Delaunay graph, see \cite[Theorem 6]{AKP21}. Thus, the primal hypergraph $H(Q,\E_{\F})$ also has a bounded VC-dimension.
	Applying the $\epsilon$-net theorem to the hypergraph $H(Q,\E_{\F})$, with $\epsilon=\frac{1}{p}$, we obtain a subset of $Q$ of size $O(p \log p)$ which is a transversal for $\F$. This completes the proof.      
\end{proof} 

In the special case where $\F$ is a family of non-piercing regions, the assertion of Theorem \ref{thm:2} can be strengthened. 

\begin{proof}[Proof of Corollary \ref{cor:3}]
First, observe that in this case the dual hypergraph of $\F$ admits a hereditarily planar, and hence $3$-linear, Delaunay graph. Indeed, the dual hypergraph $H$ of $\F$ admits a planar support (as was proved in \cite{Raman2020a} and later reproved in \cite{Dalal24}), which is a planar graph $G$ on the vertex set $\F$ such that each hyperedge of $H$ intersects a connected subgraph of $G$. Therefore, the Delaunay graph of $H$ is clearly a subgraph of a planar graph.

Second, the primal hypergraph of a family of non-piercing regions admits an $\epsilon$-net of size $O(\frac{1}{\epsilon})$, by \cite[Theorem 24]{N.Alon2022} (based on \cite{Pyrga2008a}). Therefore, the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:2} can be applied with the linear sized $\epsilon$-net, and the assertion of the corollary follows.  
\end{proof}


