	\section{Related Works}\label{sec:r-work}
	%To the best of our knowledge, Yamada et al. initiated the study of the \kp problem with a graph structure on the items (which they called conflict graph), where the goal is to compute an independent set which has the maximum total value and satisfies the budget constraint. The paper proposes a set of heuristics and upper bounds based on Lagrangean relaxation~\cite{yamada2002heuristic}. Later, Hifi and Michrafy designed a meta-heuristic approach using reactive local search techniques for the problem~\cite{hifi2006reactive,hifi2007reduction}.  Bettinelli et al.~\cite{BettinelliCM17} proposed a dynamic programming based pruning in a branching based algorithm using the upper bounds proposed by Held et al.~\cite{HeldCS12}. Coniglio et al. presented a branch-and-bound type algorithm for the problem~\cite{ConiglioFS21}. Finally, Luiz et al. proposed a cutting plane based algorithm~\cite{LuizSU21}. Pferschy and Schauer~\cite{PferschyS17}, Gurski and Rehs~\cite{GurskiR19}, and Goebbels et al.~\cite{GoebbelsGK22} showed approximation algorithms for the \kp problem with more sophisticated neighborhood constraints. Ito et al.~\cite{ito2008approximability} studied FPTASes for a generalized version of our problems, called the {\sc Maximum partition} problem but on a specialized graph class, namely the class of series-parallel graphs. Although their work mentions that their results can be extended to bounded treewidth graphs, the authors state that the algorithmic techniques for that is more complex but do not explain the techniques. To the best of our knowledge, there is no follow-up work where the techniques for bounded treewidth graphs are explained. There are many other generalizations of the basic \kp problem which have been studied extensively in the literature~\cite{martello1990knapsack,kellerer2004multidimensional,CacchianiILM22,cacchiani2022knapsack}.\\


	%\dos is the widely studied classical graph problem, which is known as \NPC for general graphs~\cite{haynes2013fundamentals}. It is polynomial time solvable for trees ~\cite{cockaynif1975linear,mitchell1979linear}, $k$-trees for fixed $k$~\cite{corneil1987dynamic}, Permutation graphs ~\cite{farber1985domination,brandstadt1985restriction,tsai1990fast}, Interval graphs~\cite{chang1998efficient}, Circular-arc graphs ~\cite{chang1998efficient}, Co-comparability graph~\cite{kratsch1993domination}, Strongly chordal graphs~\cite{farber1984domination}. And for different graph classes like $k$-trees with arbitrary vertex $k$~\cite{corneil1987dynamic}, Bipartite graph~\cite{chang1984k,bertossi1984dominating,haynes2013fundamentals}, Chordal graphs even when restricted to Undirected path graphs~\cite{booth1982dominating}, Split graphs~\cite{bertossi1984dominating}, Comparability graph~\cite{haynes2023domination}, Grid graphs~\cite{clark1990unit} and thus  for Perfect graphs \dos problem is \NPC.

	Our problem is the generalization of Weighted \dos problem (WDS), which is \NPC ~\cite{maw1997weighted}. It is polynomial time solvable for trees~\cite{natarajan1978optimum}. For strongly chordal graphs, WDS is also polynomial time solvable  ~\cite{farber1984domination}. WDS is solvable in time $\OO(n^3)$~\cite{farber1985domination} for permutation graphs. WDS takes in time $\OO(n)$ for Interval graphs and for Circular-arc graphs there are algorithm of solving WDS in time $\OO(n+m)$~\cite{chang1998efficient}.  Minimum Weight \dos  problem (MWDS) is \NP-Hard. There is greedy heuristic algorithm~\cite{lin2016effective} for Minimum Weight \dos MWDS. Most of the existing approximation algorithms focus on some special graphs like disk graph, growth bounded graphs etc. But they are not easily generalized for general graphs. An approximation algorithm (PTAS) ~\cite{wang2012ptas} (restricted to polynomial growth bounded graphs with bounded degree constraint) and metaheuristic algorithms (hybrid genetic algorithm and hybrid ant colony optimization)~\cite{potluri2013hybrid} introduced for MWDS.

	\hl{K-domination:}

	The \dos problem is \FPT, where treewidth is the parameter. If a graph $\GG$ containing $n$ vertices with tree decomposition of width at most $k$ is given then \dos problem can be solved in $4^k\cdot k^{\OO(1)}\cdot n$ and $3^k\cdot n^{\OO(1)}$ time for two different algorithms~\cite{cygan2015parameterized}. According to Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis \dos can not be solved in time $(3-\epsilon)^{tw}\cdot n^{\OO(1)}$~\cite{lokshtanov2011known}. It is also \FPT in the graph that does not contain the complete bipartite graph $k_{(1,3)}$ as an induced subgraph. ~\cite{cygan2011dominating, hermelin2011domination}. There is an algorithm which determines whether a graph \dos of fixed cardinality $k \geq 2$ in time $\OO(n^{k+o(1)})$~\cite{eisenbrand2004complexity}. For $\HH-$minor free graph $\GG$ with $n$ vertices admits polynomial kernel~\cite{fomin2012linear}. There is an \FPT algorithm for Planar \dos with complexity $\OO(8^k \cdot n)$ ~\cite{downey2013fundamentals}~\cite{alber2005refined}. \dos problem is $\WW[1]$-hard for the special class of graphs axis-parallel unit squares, intersection graphs of axis parallel rectangles, axis-parallel segments~\cite{markarian2012degree}. \dos problem is $\WW[2]$-complete, thus is unlikely to have an FPT algorithm for graphs in general~\cite{niedermeier2006invitation,cygan2015parameterized}.

	Constant factor approximation exists for planar graph to find \dos in polynomial time ~\cite{baker1994approximation}. Dominating set and Set cover are equivalent in terms of approximation ratio. One can reduce \dos to Set cover problem~\cite{paz1981non}. $\OO(\log n)$ factor approximation algorithm exists for Set cover problem ~\cite{vazirani1997approximation}. So, we can say that \dos also will be solved with $\OO(\log n)$ approximation factor. Set cover problem does not admit $(1-\epsilon)\ln n$ factor approximation in polynomial tome, where $\epsilon> 0$~\cite{dinur2014analytical}. If there is some $\epsilon> 0$ such that a polynomial time algorithm can approximate Set cover within $(1-\epsilon)ln n$, then NP $\subset$ TIME$(n^{O(log log n})$
	)~\cite{feige1998threshold}.

	The exact algorithm for \dos problem runs in time $\OO(1.5063^n)$~\cite{van2008design}. Further improvement done by the algorithm with running time upper bounded by $\OO(1.4969^n)$~\cite{van2011exact}, which is the best known result. The algorithm for Minimum \dos takes time $\OO(1.4864^n)$ with polynomial space~\cite{iwata2012faster}. The exact algorithm  for Connected \dos is solvable in time $\OO(1.9407^n)$ ~\cite{fomin2008solving}.\\

