	\begin{proof}
		We already proved that \dosknapsack is \NPC. Now we reduce an arbitrary instance of general graph $(\GG(\VV,\EE),(w(v))_{v\in\VV},(\alpha(v))_{v\in\VV})$ with \dosknapsack of size $\leq s$ to the instance of a bipartite graph $(\BB(\hat{\VV},\hat{\EE}),(w(v))_{v\in\hat{\VV}},(\alpha(v))_{v\in\hat{\VV}})$ that also has a \dosknapsack of size $\leq s+1$. The reduction is as follows:
		\begin{align*}
			&\hat{\VV} = X \cup Y\\  
			&X=\VV(=\{v_i : \forall i\in[1,n]\}) \cup \{x\}\\
			&Y=\VV'(=\{v_i' : v_i\in \VV, \forall i\in[1,n]\})\cup\{y\}\\
			&\hat{\EE} = \{\{v_i,v_i'\}\cup \{\{v_i, v_j'\}\cup \{v_i', v_j\}:\{v_i,v_j\} \in\EE\} \cup  \{x,v_i'\} \cup \{x,y\}: i\neq j, \forall i, j\in[1,n]\}\\
			&w(v_i)=w(v_i') = 1, \alpha(v_i)=\alpha(v_i')=1 \quad \forall i\in[1,n]\\
			&w(x)=w(y) = 1, \alpha(x)=\alpha(y)=1%\{\alpha(v_k): \alpha(v_k)>\alpha(v_i), i\neq k, \forall i, k\in[1,n]\}
		\end{align*}
		Now, we can proof that the arbitrary graph $\GG$ has a \dosknapsack of size $\leq s$ iff the corresponding bipartite graph $\BB$ has a \dosknapsack of size $\leq s+1$.\\
		\noindent
		Suppose $\GG$ has a \dosknapsack $\DD$ of size $\leq s$. So, $\sum_{v\in \DD} w(v)=s$ and  $\sum_{v\in \DD} \alpha(v)=s$ otherwise profit would be $< s$. Now, either $\{x\}\cup \DD$ or $\{y\}\cup \DD$ must be the \dos for $\BB$. Because, $\DD$ dominates all the vertices $\VV$ and $\VV'$ and we need to choose any one of $x,y$ for the edge $\{x,y\}$. Thus, $B$ has \dosknapsack $\DD$ of size $\leq s+1$.\\
		Conversely, suppose $B$ has \dosknapsack $\hat{\DD}$ of size $\leq s+1$. If a vertex $u \notin \hat{\DD}\cap \VV$ then $\VV'\subset\hat{\DD}$, otherwise some vertices $u\in \VV'$ would be undominated. Hence, the corresponding vertices $v \in \VV$ of $v' \in \VV'$ and $\hat{\DD}\cap \VV$ forms the \dosknapsack of $\GG$ of size $s$ as $\hat{\DD}$ contains any one of $x$ and $y$. If a node $u \in \hat{\DD}\cap \VV$ the $\VV$ is the \dosknapsack of $\GG$ of size $s$. 	\textcolor{red}{[Check converse part]}
	\end{proof}

