		\begin{theorem}\label{thm:shortest-algo-Bellman-Ford}
			There is an algorithm for \shortestpathknapsack running in time $\OO(mn\cdot\min\{s^2,(\alpha(\VV))^2\})$, where $m$ is the number of edges in the input graph.
		\end{theorem}

		\begin{proof}
			Let $(\GG=(\VV,\EE,(c(e))_{e\in\EE}),(w(u))_{u\in\VV},(\alpha(u))_{u\in\VV},s,d,x,y)$ be an arbitrary instance of \shortestpathknapsack. We design another dynamic-programming based algorithm for this problem. For every vertex $v\in \VV$, we formally define $D_v=\{(w,\alpha):\exists$ an $x$ to $v$ shortest-path \PP such that $w(\PP)=w \leq w(\QQ)$ and/or $\alpha(\PP)=\alpha \geq \alpha(\QQ)$, for every other $x$ to $v$ shortest-path $\QQ\}$. That is, we store undominated weight-value pairs of all shortest $x$ to $v$ paths in $D_v$. We define $\delta_v$ as the distance of $v$ from $x$.  We initialize $ \delta_x=0, D_x=\{(w(x),\alpha(x))\}, \delta_{v'}=\infty,$ and $ D_{v'}=\emptyset$ for every $v'\in\VV\setminus\{x\}$.


			Updating DP table: We pick an edge $\{z,u\} \in \EE$ and examine if $\delta_u>\delta_z+c(\{z,u\})$ is \true, then we set $\delta_u=\delta_z+c(\{z,u\})$ by resetting $D_u=\emptyset$; else, if $\delta_u=\delta_z+c(\{z,u\})$ is \true, then we set $\delta_u=\delta_z+c(\{z,u\})$ without resetting $D_u=\emptyset$; no change, otherwise. Now, we update $D_u$ as follows: for every pair $(w,\alpha)\in D_z$, we update $D_u$ to $(D_u\cup\{(w+w(u),\alpha+\alpha(u))\})$ if $w+w(u)\le s$. We remove all dominated pairs from $D_u$. In each iteration of the  total $(|\VV|-1)$ iterations, we execute these steps for every edge belongs to $ \EE$. After $(|\VV|-1)$ iterations, for each edge $\{z,u\} \in \EE$ recheck the $\delta_u>\delta_z+c(\{z,u\})$, if it satisfied for any one of the edge $\in \EE$ the algorithm returns \no solution because of the presence of a negative-weight cycle which is reachable from $x$; otherwise it outputs \yes and returns a pair $(w,\alpha)\in D_y$ such that $w\le s$ and $\alpha\ge d$.\\

			Correctness of our algorithm: From the proof of Bellman-Ford algorithm ~\cite{DBLP:books/daglib/0023376}, we observe that if the graph $\GG$ does not contain any negative-weight cycle which reachable from $x$ algorithm outputs \yes and gives the shortest distance from $x$ to $v$ is $\delta_v$ for any vertex $v\in\VV$; if $\GG$ contains a negative-weight cycle reachable from $x$, it returns \no. Now, we claim that if the algorithm outputs \yes, i.e., there is no negative-weight cycle reachable from $x$, for every vertex $v \in \VV$, $(k_1, k_2) \in D_v$ if and only if there exists an $x$ to $v$ undominated shortest path $\PP$ such that $w(\PP)=k_1$ and $\alpha(\PP)=k_2$.\\
			Let $\GG$ has no negative-weight cycle and $\PP=x,\ldots,z,v$ be an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$ such that $\{z,v\}$ is an edge. For one direction, we want to prove that if $w(\PP)=k_1$ and $\alpha(\PP)=k_2$ then $(k_1,k_2) \in D_v$. Before proving this we first show that $\PP$ is an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$. Let $\PP_1'=x,\ldots,z$ is an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $z$. By the proof of correctness of Bellman-Ford algorithm~\cite{DBLP:books/daglib/0023376}, $\PP_1'$ is a shortest path from $x$ to $z$. To prove that $\PP_1'$ is an undominated shortest path, if possible let $\PP_1'$ is not an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $z$. Then $\exists$ another shortest path $\PP_2'$ from $x$ to $z$ such that $\PP_2'$ dominates $\PP_1'$. So, there exists a shortest path $\PP'$ from $x$ to $v$ (which is $\PP_2'$ followed by $v$) that dominates $\PP$, which contradicts our assumption that $\PP$ is an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$. Hence, $\PP_1'=x,\ldots,z$ is an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $z$. Thus, $\PP$ is an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$ without depending the positive or negative edge-weight of the edge $\{z,v\}$.\\
			Now, let $\GG$ has no negative-weight cycle and $\PP=x,\ldots,z,v$ be an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$ such that $w(\PP)=k_1$ and $\alpha(\PP)=k_2$ then we prove by induction that $(k_1,k_2) \in D_v$. If $\PP$ contains only the source vertex $x$ then trivially the hypothesis is \true. %Now, let $v$ has $i$ number of predecessors: $z, z_1, z_2, \ldots z_i$, where $v \neq x$ and $1\leq i \leq n-1$. Let us suppose the hypothesis is \true for all predecessors of $v$. \\
			Now, let the hypothesis is \true for $\PP_1=x,\ldots,z$. That is, $\exists$ an undominated shortest path $\PP_1$ from $x$ to $z$ such that $(w(\PP_1),\alpha(\PP_1)) \in D_z$. Then according to the algorithm, for the edge $\{z,v\}$ we get $w(\PP)=w(\PP_1)+w(v)$ and $\alpha(\PP)=\alpha(\PP_1)+\alpha(v)$. Let $w(\PP)=k_1$ and $\alpha(\PP)=k_2$. If possible consider $(k_1,k_2) \notin D_v$. That indicates that either $D_v=\emptyset$ or $D_v$ contains pairs, other than $(w(\PP)=k_1,\alpha(\PP)=k_2)$. We can argue that $D_v=\emptyset$ is not possible because $(w(\PP_1),\alpha(\PP_1)) \in D_z$ and there is an edge between $z$ and $v$. Hence, $D_v$ must be a non-empty set. Let $(k_1', k_2') \in D_v$ other than $(k_1, k_2)$ where $k_1=w(\PP)$, $k_2=\alpha(\PP)$. Then there must be another path ,say, $\PP_2$ from $x$ to $z$ such that $k_1'=w(\PP_2)+w(v)$ and $k_2'=\alpha(\PP_2)+\alpha(v)$ that dominates the pair $(k_1, k_2)$, which is a contradiction of the hypothesis that $\PP_1$ ia an undominated shortest path from $x$ to $z$ such that $(w(\PP_1),\alpha(\PP_1)) \in D_z$. Thus, $(k_1,k_2) \in D_v$.

			\noindent
			Conversely let, $(k_1, k_2) \in D_v$ be an arbitrary undominated pair in absence of negative-weight cycle reachable from $x$. If $v=x$ then $w(x)=k_1$ and $\alpha(x)= k_2$. Thus, the hypothesis holds for $x$. Let $x\neq v$, $z$ is the predecessor of $v$ and the hypothesis is true for $z$. That is, $\exists$ undominated pair $(k_1', k_2')\in D_z$ such that  $w(\PP_1)=k_1'$ and $\alpha(\PP_1)= k_2'$, where $\PP_1=x,\ldots,z$. Then $D_v\neq \emptyset$. According to the correctness of Bellman-Ford algorithm $\delta_v =\delta_z+c(\{z,v\})$. Hence, the path $\PP=x,\ldots,z,v$ shortest path from $x$ to $v$ and $k_1'+w(v)=k_1$, $k_2'+\alpha(v)=k_2$. We have shown earlier that there is no other shortest path $\PP_2$ possible from $x,\ldots,z$ such that $w(\PP_2)=k_1'$ and $\alpha(\PP_2)= k_2'$. Thus, $\PP=x,\ldots,z,v$ is the undominated shortest path from $x$ to $v$ such that $w(\PP)=k_1$ and $\alpha(\PP)=k_2$.\\

			For every vertex $v\in\VV$, the cardinality of $D_v$ is at most $s$ and also at most $\alpha(\VV)$ and thus at most $\min\{s,\alpha(\VV)\}$. The above algorithm executed $n-1$ times for each edge. Thus the running time of the algorithm is $\OO(mn\cdot\min\{s^2,(\alpha(\VV))^2\})$, where $n,m$ are the number of vertices and edges in the input graph respectively.


		\end{proof}
