\begin{abstract}
Gaps between established security standards and their practical implementation have the potential to introduce vulnerabilities% in 5G/6G systems
, possibly exposing them to security risks. To effectively address and mitigate these security and compliance challenges, security risk management strategies are essential. 
%Effective risk management is crucial for addressing and mitigating security and compliance issues. 
However, it must adhere to well-established strategies and industry standards to ensure consistency, reliability, and compatibility both within and across organizations. 
%
%Security risk management is essential to effectively address and mitigate these security risks and compliance with industry standards while developing such risk management strategies to ensure consistency, reliability, and compatibility within and across organizations. 
In this paper, we introduce a new hybrid risk assessment framework called TELSAFE, which employs probabilistic modeling for quantitative risk assessment and eliminates the influence of expert opinion bias. 
%
%By employing probabilistic modeling, TELSAFE provides a quantitative risk assessment that minimizes expert opinion bias%, achieving a validation accuracy of 99.32\% against known exploited vulnerabilities
 The framework encompasses both qualitative and quantitative assessment phases, facilitating effective risk management strategies tailored to the unique requirements of %telecommunications 
organizations. A specific use case utilizing Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE)-related data demonstrates the framework's applicability and implementation in real-world scenarios, such as in the telecommunications industry.
%
%Moreover, a specific use case to demonstrate the application of the proposed framework on a dataset by leveraging CVE-related information due to the lack of publicly available data on logged security incidents or maintained risk registers has also been presented. 
%
%The overall validation results demonstrate that the proposed framework computes risk scores with an accuracy of 99.32\% when compared to the known exploited vulnerabilities list. This work serves as an initial step toward effective risk assessment in the telecommunications industry.

%The comparison of the proposed methodology, i.e., the use of event trees, with machine learning based methodolgy reveals that both methods yield an equal accuracy, however, the processing time of the proposed method is considerably less than the other, there is no additional attack surface due to the proposed approach, and the proposed technique does not require labels. This work serves as an initial step toward effective risk assessment in the telecommunications industry.
\end{abstract}

