This step includes the verification of the model and/or the results computed by the developed model. The most significant challenge when devising a risk assessment framework is the unavailability of a labeled dataset, i.e., a dataset containing ground truth values with the same emphasis as the organization implementing the said risk assessment framework. Moreover, the security incident log or risk register data of organizations is not publicly available \cite{sys60}. It hinders the feasibility of risk score validation through traditional ways, i.e., through comparing the results with the established ground truth. Therefore, conventional validation methods relying on labels or ground truth assessments may not be applicable.
In the absence of the desired ground truth for the designed risk model, this step includes checking the risk model itself. Model checking is a formal verification technique that automatically verifies whether a system meets a specified property by thoroughly exploring all reachable states within the system model, e.g., by employing symbolic model checking using NuSMV and probabilistic model checking using PRISM \cite{sys61}.
