\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}
%As mentioned earlier in Section \ref{sec:validation}, 
The data maintained by an organization (e.g., telecommunications operator) related to the logged security incidents or recorded risk registers is not available publicly \cite{sys60}. Accordingly, the dataset employed for this use case is taken from \emph{kaggle}\footnote{https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/francescomanzoni/vulnerability-management-datasets}, %\emph{CVE, CISA KEV \& EPSS Datasets}, 
which consolidates data from (i) NVD, (ii) EPSS, and (iii) CISA-KEV(Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency-Known Exploited Vulnerabilities). The said dataset contains 155,578 samples of vulnerability data (i.e., 154,528 samples for vulnerabilities that were not exploited and 1,050 samples for exploited vulnerabilities). %Due to a significant imbalance in the dataset, it has been modified to include all 1,050 CVEs that were exploited along with a proportion (randomnly selected) of the CVEs that were not exploited consisting of 1,050 samples. The values shown in Figure \ref{revised_usecase_implementation} have been computed employing this modified dataset including 2,100 samples in total.
Please note that this data has not been acquired by the source as data for gaps between standards and implementation, however, it can be linked to the gaps as the reported CVEs include vulnerabilities reported due to these gaps, e.g., CVE-2017-5638 and CVE-2020-0601 included in the said CVE dataset were created after vulnerabilities due to gaps in coding practices and cryptographic standards
respectively were discovered (refer to Figure \ref{fig:gap_to_CVE}).


