% \noindent\textit{Graph Theoretic Definitions and Notations:} 
For a \textit{graph} $\Gamma$, we write $V(\Gamma)$ and $E(\Gamma)$ to denote the vertex set and edge set, respectively.
%\textcolor{red}{We write only $V$ and $E$ when the underlying graph is clear from the context.} 
We denote an \textit{undirected edge} between two vertices $u$ and $v$ by $\{u,v\}$ and a \textit{directed edge} from $u$ to $v$ by $(u,v)$. Unless otherwise specified, the graphs we consider are simple undirected graphs. The \textit{neighbourhood} of a vertex $u$ in a graph $\G$ is denoted by $N_{\G}(u)$. For a subset $L \subseteq V(\G)$, $N(L)=\{v \in V(\G) : \text{ there exists } a \in L \text{ with } \{a,v\} \in E(\G) \}$. %the set $\{v \in V(\G) : \{u,v\} \in E \}$. 
%The \textit{out-degree} of $u$ in a directed graph $\DG$ is the size of the \textit{out-neighbourhood} of $u$, i.e., the set $\{w \in V(\DG) \ | \ (u,w) \in E(\DG)\}$ and the \textit{in-degree} of $u$ in $\DG$ is the size of the set $\{w \in V(\DG) \ | \ (w,u) \in E(\DG)\}$. 
Two vertices $u$ and $v$ are \textit{closed twins} in a \emph{directed graph} $\DG$ if their closed out-neighbourhoods are the same. %, i.e., $\{w \in V(\DG) \ | \ (u,w) \in E(\DG)\} \cup \{u\}=\{z \in V(\DG) \ | \ (v,z) \in E(\DG)\} \cup \{v\}$. 
The relation between two vertices of being closed twins is an equivalence relation, and the equivalence class under this relation is called the \textit{closed twin-class}. %the set $\{v \in V(\DG) : (u,v) \in E(\DG)\}$.  
%A \textit{connected component} of a graph $\Gamma$ is a maximal connected subgraph of $\Gamma$. %Two vertices are said to be in a \textit{connected component} if there exists a path between them.
The \textit{subgraph induced on the set} $S\subseteq V(\G)$ is denoted by $\Gamma[S]$. %If $S\subseteq V(\Gamma)$, then $\Gamma[S]$ denotes the \textit{subgraph induced on the set} $S$. 
A function $f:V(\G) \xrightarrow[]{} V(\G')$ is an \textit{isomorphism} between two graphs $\G$ and $\G'$ if $f$ is a bijection %between $V(\G)$ and $V(\G')$
such that $\{u,v\} \in E(\G)$ if and only if $\{f(u),f(v)\} \in E(\G')$; here, $\G$ and $\G'$ are called \textit{isomorphic} and it is denoted by $\G \cong \G'$. We write $col(v)$ to denote the colour of a vertex $v$ in a vertex-coloured graph. For two vertex-coloured graphs $\G$ and $\G'$, if for an isomorphism $f$, %also preserves the colour, i.e., 
$col(u)=col(f(u))$, for all $u\in V(\G)$, then $f$ is called a \textit{colour-preserving isomorphism} (or, \emph{colour-isomorphism}) and $\G$ and $\G'$ are said to be \textit{colour-isomorphic}, denoted by $\G \cong_c \G'$. %The \textit{strong product}\jinia{shift it to extended preli} of two graphs $\G$ and $\G'$ is the graph with vertex set $V(\G) \times V(\G')$ and there is an edge between $(u,u')$ and $(v,v')$ if one of the following occurs: (i) $u=v$ and $\{u',v'\}\in E(\G')$, (ii) $\{u,v\} \in E(\G)$ and $u'=v'$, (iii) $\{u,v\} \in E(\G)$ and $\{u',v'\}\in E(\G')$.

% \noindent \textit{Group Theoretic Definitions and Notations:} 
The basic definitions and facts on group theory can be found in any standard book (e.g.,  \cite{rotman2012introduction}). %In this paper, we only consider \emph{finite groups}.  
%A \textit{subgroup} $H$ of $G$ is denoted by $H \leq G$, and a \textit{normal subgroup} $H$ of $G$ is denoted by $H \lhd G$. %is denoted that $H$ is a normal subgroup of $G$. 
The \textit{order of a group} $G$ and the \textit{order of an element} $g$ in $G$ are denoted by $|G|$  and $ord(g)$ respectively. The \textit{cyclic subgroup generated by $g$} is denoted by $\langle g\rangle$, where $g$ is a \textit{generator} of $\langle g \rangle$. The number %$|gen(\langle g \rangle)|$ 
of generators of $\langle g \rangle$ is $\phi(|\langle g \rangle|)$, where $\phi$ is the \textit{Euler's totient function} and $\phi(p_1^{\alpha_1}\dots p_k^{\alpha_k})=p_1^{\alpha_1-1}(p_1-1)\dots p_k^{\alpha_k-1}(p_k-1)$, where $p_i$'s are distinct primes. Any cyclic group of order $n$ is isomorphic to $\ZZ_n$, the additive group of integers modulo $n$. The \textit{exponent} of a finite group is the least common multiple of the orders of all elements of the group.

