A group $G$ is called a \textit{$p$-group} if the order of $G$ is a power of a prime $p$. 
It is common to refer to a group as a $p$-group whenever its order is a power of some prime, where the symbol $p$ denotes an arbitrary prime. 
If $p^m$ is the highest power of a prime $p$ such that $p^m$ divides $|G|$, then there is at least one subgroup of order $p^m$ in $G$, and such a subgroup is called \textit{Sylow p-subgroup} of $G$. A finite group is called a \textit{nilpotent group} if it is a direct product of its Sylow subgroups. Moreover, each Sylow subgroup is unique in a finite nilpotent group.

