Let $\mathscr{G}$ be a class of graphs closed under isomorphism. We consider problems whose instances are of the form $(\Gamma_1,\ldots, \Gamma_k,x)$, where $\Gamma_i\in \mathscr{G}$, $k$ is a constant,  and  $||x||=O(\log s)$, where $s=||(\Gamma_1,\ldots, \Gamma_k,x)||$, is the size of the instance. In other words, a language representation of such problems is a subset of $\mathscr{G}^k\times \{0,1\}^*$, where $k$ is a constant, and the last component (i.e., $x$) is of logarithmic length in the length of the input. We call such a language $L$ \emph{isomorphism invariant over} $\mathscr{G}$, if for all $\Lambda_i,\Gamma_i\in \mathscr{G}$, where $1\leq i \leq k$ %$\Lambda_1,\ldots, \Lambda_k, \Gamma_1,\ldots, \Gamma_k\in \mathscr{G}$ 
such that $\Gamma_i\cong \Lambda_i$, %\ldots, \Gamma_k\cong \Lambda_k$ 
for all $i$, we have $(\Gamma_1,\ldots,\Gamma_k,s)\in L$ if and only if $(\Lambda_1,\ldots,\Lambda_k,s)\in L$. A wide range of problems are isomorphism invariant. For example, Graph Colouring: The language is $L=\{(\Gamma,r)|\Gamma \textrm{ has a vertex colouring with } r\textrm{ colours}\}$, where $r$ is given in binary and %. Note that the length of $r$, 
$||r||=O( \log |V(\Gamma)|)$. This problem is isomorphism invariant because $\Gamma$ is $r$-colourable if and only if an isomorphic copy of $\Gamma$ is $r$-colourable. %Another example is the Subgraph Isomorphim problem $L=\{(\Gamma_1,\Gamma_2)|\Gamma_1 \textrm{ is a subgraph of } \Gamma_2\}$. In this problem, the $x$ part is the empty string $\varepsilon$.
Similarly, the following problems are isomorphism invariant: Independent Set, Clique, Graph Colouring, Hamiltonian Path, Partition into Cliques, Oriented Diameter, etc. 

% Let $\mathscr{G}$ denote a class of graphs closed under isomorphism. We consider problems with instances of the form $(\Gamma_1, \ldots, \Gamma_k, x)$, where $\Gamma_i \in \mathscr{G}$, $k$ is a fixed constant, and $||x|| = O(\log s)$, with $s = ||(\Gamma_1, \ldots, \Gamma_k, x)||$. That is, the language is a subset of $\mathscr{G}^k \times {0,1}^*$ with logarithmic-length auxiliary input. We say that a language $L$ is isomorphism invariant over $\mathscr{G}$ if for all $\Gamma_i, \Lambda_i \in \mathscr{G}$ with $\Gamma_i \cong \Lambda_i$ for each $i$, it holds that $(\Gamma_1, \ldots, \Gamma_k, x) \in L$ if and only if $(\Lambda_1, \ldots, \Lambda_k, x) \in L$. Many classical problems fall into this framework. For example, Graph Colouring corresponds to the language $L = {(\Gamma, r) \mid \Gamma \text{ admits a proper } r\text{-colouring}}$, where $r$ is given in binary and $||r|| = O(\log |V(\Gamma)|)$. Since colourability is preserved under isomorphism, the problem is isomorphism invariant. Another example is Subgraph Isomorphism, defined by $L = {(\Gamma_1, \Gamma_2) \mid \Gamma_1 \subseteq \Gamma_2}$, where $x = \varepsilon$. Similar reasoning applies to problems such as Independent Set, Clique, Hamiltonian Path, Partition into Cliques, and Oriented Diameter.



The following theorem is stated for isomorphism invariant problems on power graphs. However, the theorem also holds for directed power graphs, enhanced power graphs, commuting graphs, and bounded-degree Cayley graphs.

\medskip 

\begin{theorem}\label{iso-inv}
    If $L$ is an isomorphism invariant graph problem over the class of power graphs, then $L$ is not NP-complete under ETH.
\end{theorem}

We prove the above theorem using a version of Mahaney's theorem \cite{mahaney1982sparse}, which can be proved easily by mimicking the proof of Mahaney's theorem (see, e.g., \cite{grochow2016np}). We say that a language $L$ is \emph{quasipolynomially sparse} if there exists a constant $c>0$ such that $|L\cap \{0,1\}^n|=2^{O(\log^c n)}$. 

\medskip 

\begin{theorem}\label{maha}
Under ETH, no quasipolynomially sparse language is NP-complete.
\end{theorem}

