\begin{remark}
    A similar statement to \Cref{Graph motif not in P} can be made without using ETH. More explicitly, \textsc{Graph Motif} on power graphs cannot be solved in quasipolynomial time, unless $\mathsf{EXP}=\mathsf{NEXP}$. This is because, in that case \Cref{sat to pow} would imply that $\mathsf{NP} \subseteq \bigcup_{t>0}\mathsf{DTIME}(2^{\log ^tn)})$, which in turn would imply that $\mathsf{EXP}=\mathsf{NEXP}$ \cite{10.1007/3-540-56287-7_99}.
    %in other words, the hardness of \textsc{Graph Motif} in power graphs of cyclic groups lies outside of ETH. If \textsc{Graph Motif} on power graphs of cyclic groups could be solved in quasipolynomial time, then \Cref{sat to pow} implies that $EXP=NEXP$ \cite{10.1007/3-540-56287-7_99}. This violates the widely held belief that $EXP\neq NEXP$.
\end{remark}



We now show the existence of an algorithm with runtime $2^{o(n)}$ for power graphs of cyclic groups. We begin with a few definitions and a relevant result.
