    Two vertices $v$ and $v'$ of a graph are said to have the same \emph{type} if and only if $N(v)\setminus\{v'\} = N(v')\setminus \{v\}$. A graph $\Gamma(V,E)$ has \emph{neighbourhood diversity} at most $w$ if there exists a partition of $V(\G)$ into at most $w$ sets, such that all the vertices in each set have the same type \cite{lampis2012algorithmic}.
%\end{definition} 
