Given a directed graph $\DG$, we construct a directed graph $R(\DG)$ from $\DG$, referred to as the \emph{reduced graph}, by the following method: (1) For each closed twin-class $\tau$ of $R(\DG)$,  we create a vertex in $\DG$; (2) The colour of each vertex $\tau$, denoted by $col(\tau)$, of $R(\DG)$ is the out-degree of any vertex of $\tau$ in $\DG$; (3) Put a directed edge from $\tau_1$ to $\tau_2$ in $R(\DG)$ where $\tau_1 \neq \tau_2$, if there is a directed edge from some vertex $x_1 \in \tau_1$ to some vertex $x_2 \in \tau_2$ in $\DG$. %and $col(T_1)=col(T_2).p$, where $p$ is some prime.

 Recall that for any finite group $G$, the out-degree of a vertex $u$ in $DPow(G)$ equals to $ord(u)$ in $G$. If $(u,v)$ is an edge in $DPow(G)$, then $ord(v) \mid ord(u)$. Hence, if $(\tau_1,\tau_2)$ is an edge in $R(DPow(G))$, then $col(\tau_2) \mid col(\tau_1)$. So, it is easy to see that $R(DPow(G))$ is a directed acyclic graph (DAG). In $DPow(G)$, two vertices $u$ and $v$ are closed twins if and only if $\langle u \rangle =\langle v \rangle $ in $G$, i.e., $u$ and $v$ are two generators of the same cyclic subgroup in $G$. So, the vertex set of  $R(DPow(G))$ is $\{[x] \ | \ x \in G \}$. If $(x,y)$ is an edge in $DPow(G)$, then for every $x'\in [x]$ and for every $y' \in [y]$, there is an edge $(x',y') \in DPow(G)$. Then by \Cref{remark power graph}, there is an edge $(\tau_1,\tau_2)$ in $R(DPow(G))$. %\textcolor{blue}{Due to these properties, if a directed graph is an outcome of the reconstruction algorithm by \cite{das_et_al:LIPIcs.FSTTCS.2024.20}, the reduction process is unambiguous and well-defined.} 
