\begin{abstract}\small
%Since AI algorithms incur high computational cost, reducing the bit-width of various data formats to alleviate this burden has been actively explored. 
%AI applications employ different precision configurations and data types to meet various design requirements (e.g., area, power, and accuracy constraints), depending on the devices and tasks. 
%To efficiently support these versatile workloads on AI accelerators, the accelerators need to provide bit-level flexibility for diverse data formats. 
In this work, we introduce an area- and energy-efficient multiply-accumulate (MAC) unit, named \textit{\underline{Jack Unit}}, that is a jack-of-all-trades, supporting various data formats such as integer (INT), floating point (FP), and microscaling data format (MX). 
%The proposed MAC unit 
It provides bit-level flexibility and enhances hardware efficiency by i) replacing the carry-save multiplier (CSM) in the FP multiplier with a precision-scalable CSM, ii) performing the adjustment of significands based on the exponent differences within the CSM, and iii) utilizing 2D sub-word parallelism. 
%To verify the practical effectiveness, 
To assess effectiveness, we implemented the layout of the Jack unit and three baseline MAC units.
%For the architectural evaluation, 
Additionally, we designed an AI accelerator equipped with our Jack units to compare with a state-of-the-art AI accelerator supporting various data formats. 
The proposed MAC unit occupies 1.17$\sim$2.01$\times$ smaller area and consumes 1.05$\sim$1.84$\times$ lower power compared to the baseline MAC units. 
On five AI benchmarks, the accelerator designed with our Jack units improves energy efficiency by 1.32$\sim$5.41$\times$ over the baseline across various data formats.
