\textbf{Attack Evaluation Metric.}  The attacker has to devise specific strategies to impair these commonly used  metrics: (1) \textbf{Qerror metric}: Defined as $Q(est(\textbf{q}|E), C_D(\textbf{q}))=max(\frac{est(\textbf{q}|E)}{C_D(\textbf{q})},\frac{C_D(\textbf{q})}{est(\textbf{q}|E)})$, where $est(\textbf{q}|E)$ is the estimators $E$'s prediction on given query \textbf{q}. It measures the distance between the estimated cardinality $est(\textbf{q}|E)$ and the true cardinality $C_D(\textbf{q})$ of a query. (2) \textbf{End-to-end latency}: Measures the total latency  for generating a plan with estimated cardinalities and executing the physical plan. \textcolor{black}{This metric is essential for demonstrating how an estimator can improve query optimization performance. It serves as a gold standard for assessing the effectiveness of CE approaches~\cite{DBMSCE,FactorJoin,ALECE,ASM}}. 

