\section{Conclusion}
This study reveals critical vulnerabilities of learned cardinality estimators to minimal data-level drifts. Through a black-box data-centric algorithmic complexity attack, we demonstrate that even slight training data modifications can severely degrade estimator accuracy. We prove the NP-Hardness of finding optimal attack strategies and develop an efficient $(1-\kappa)$-approximation algorithm with polynomial-time complexity. Experiments on STATS-CEB and IMDB-JOB benchmarks show our attack's effectiveness: modifying merely 0.8\% of database tuples causes a 1000$\times$ increase in 90-th percentile Qerror and up to 20$\times$ longer processing times. These findings expose the fragility of current estimators in real-world scenarios. We also propose practical countermeasures against such black-box attacks, providing valuable insights for developing robust learned optimizers. Future work could focus on creating more resilient estimation techniques that can withstand data-level disruptions while maintaining reliable performance.\looseness=-1

% In this study, we have unveiled the significant vulnerabilities of all learned cardinality estimators to minimal data-level drifts. By launching a black-box data-centric algorithmic complexity attack, we demonstrated that even slight modifications to the training data can severely undermine the accuracy of these estimators. We established the NP-Hardness of identifying optimal attack strategies and responded with a specialized approximation algorithm that efficiently approaches near-optimal solutions within polynomial-time, achieving a $(1-\kappa)$ approximation ratio. Our comprehensive experiments on well-known benchmarks, including STATS-CEB and IMDB-JOB, validated the potency of our attack. Remarkably, altering just 1\% of the tuples in the database led to a thousandfold increase in the 90-th percentile Qerror of all estimators and up to a twentyfold escalation in end-to-end processing time. These findings underscore a common fragility of current learned  estimators and highlight the critical risks they pose for real-world deployment. Moving forward, it is crucial to develop more robust estimation techniques that can effectively handle data-level disruptions, thereby ensuring reliable performance in practical deployments.\looseness=-1

