\begin{abstract}
% 数据安全至关重要，面对海量的业务流量，检测命中规则的异常流量以防止恶意攻击是一件具有挑战性的工作。检测命中规则的异常流量面临以下三方面的问题：为了最大化拦截率，需要在有限资源下设计高效算法和策略；寻找与特定流量对应的规则是典型的NP难度问题；获取最大拦截流量的目标具有黑盒优化的性质。为了解决以上问题，本文将原始问题的核验规则编码成可满足性问题，并基于贝叶斯优化设计了专用的DPLL SAT求解器进行求解。实验表明，使用本文提出的算法能够对于人工构造的算例和真实业务场景算例获取拦截率很高的解，从而指导生成更完善的核验规则，并且我们还通过实验分析评估了算法中三种随机策略的重要性。

% Payment platforms probably generate billions of transactions every day, involving trillions of funds flowing among users and related institutions. Any slight mistake could lead to significant funds loss. Usually, the experts manually build verification rules to identify the transactions potentially causing funds loss. However, the rules written by experts may have vulnerabilities, where the financial loss caused by the malicious transactions is not avoided appropriately. Hence, it is crucial to develop a vulnerability-detection system aiming to find the vulnerabilities of existing verification rules and generate new verification rules accordingly if necessary. In this paper, we design a novel vulnerability-detection system by proposing SAT-Based Bayesian Optimization (SAT-BO) algorithm. Firstly, vulnerability-detection problems are formulated as Boolean satisfiability (SAT) problems and the corresponding SAT solutions are proved to be vulnerabilities of existing verification rules. Meanwhile, the SAT solutions can be decoded to verification rules easily. Secondly, we define a metric reflecting the importance of the SAT solutions,i.e.,vulnerability coverage, which relfects the vulnerability afftect range. And we propose to utilize the Bayesian Optimization(BO) to guide the SAT solver to SAT solutions with higher. Finally, experiment results show that our proposed algorithm can obtain solutions with high interception rates, which verifies the effectiveness of SAT-BO on the generation of more comprehensive verification rules.

% From GPT4-turbo-128K
Electronic payment platforms are estimated to process billions of transactions daily, with the cumulative value of these transactions potentially reaching into the trillions. Even a minor error within this high-volume environment could precipitate substantial financial losses. To mitigate this risk, manually constructed verification rules, developed by domain experts, are typically employed to identify and scrutinize transactions in production environments. However, due to the absence of a systematic approach to ensure the robustness of these verification rules against vulnerabilities, they remain susceptible to exploitation. 

To mitigate this risk, manually constructed verification rules, developed by domain experts, are typically employed to identify and scrutinize transactions in production environments. However, due to the absence of a systematic approach to ensure the robustness of these verification rules against vulnerabilities, they remain susceptible to exploitation. To ensure data security, database maintainers usually compose complex verification rules to check whether a query/update request is valid. However, the rules written by experts are usually imperfect, and malicious requests may bypass these rules. As a result, the demand for identifying the defects of the rules systematically emerges. To address this challenging problem, we designed a SAT-Based Bayesian Optimization (SAT-BO) algorithm. SAT-BO encodes the problem into Boolean satisfiability problems (SAT) and searches for preferred truth value assignment by a customized DPLL algorithm. Based on the above SAT solver, we utilize a Bayesian optimization algorithm to determine the preference of variable assignment which corresponds to a large amount of requests. Experiment results show that our proposed algorithm can obtain solutions with high interception rates on several simulated scenarios, which verifies the effectiveness of SAT-BO on the generation of more comprehensive verification rules. In addition, we evaluate and justify the importance of three independent variable flipping strategies by experimental analysis.
% Consequently, malicious transactions may circumvent these expert-devised rules, resulting in significant financial detriment.
